## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; font-family: 'Arial Black', sans-serif; font-size: 28px; border-radius: 8px;">
    Lesson Planner – Theme # Preserving Our Heritage – Lesson # 2
</div>
<div style="background-color: #f0f0f0; color: #333; padding: 10px; text-align: center; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 10px;">
    <i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Preserving Our Heritage</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning How We Take Care of Our Past</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Recognize the writer's point of view in various texts. Reading: Analyze authentic non-fiction texts.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can recognize a writer's point of view in texts on heritage preservation and explain their reasoning with textual evidence (e.g., "The writer argues that historical traditions are crucial for national identity because they strengthen community pride."). Can analyze articles on cultural heritage, identifying examples that highlight preservation efforts (e.g., "Restoring Casco Viejo attracts tourists and promotes local culture."). Can summarize main points and viewpoints in articles about heritage, preparing for classroom presentations (e.g., "The author argues that preserving Casco Viejo is essential for maintaining Panama's cultural legacy and historical identity." "Another viewpoint emphasizes the importance of protecting indigenous traditions, such as the craftsmanship of the Guna molas, to ensure that future generations value and celebrate their cultural roots.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e0e0;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images showing different aspects of Panamanian cultural heritage (e.g., Casco Viejo, Guna molas, Emberá villages, Panama Canal). Students are asked to identify the places/traditions and briefly discuss what they represent. The teacher asks: "What do all these images have in common?" (Expected answer: They are all part of Panama's <i>heritage</i> and <i>history</i>.) "Why is <i>preserving</i> these sites and traditions important for our national <i>identity</i>?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces the concept of "writer's point of view." The teacher states, "Today, we will focus on reading texts and understanding what the writer thinks about the topic, not just what the text says." The teacher briefly models how to spot opinion words or phrases in a very short sample sentence (e.g., "It is <i>critical</i> to restore the old buildings" – showing that "critical" expresses a strong viewpoint). The teacher also models the pronunciation of key vocabulary like "heritage" /ˈher.ɪ.tɪdʒ/ (practicing reduced vowels) and "became" /bɪˈkeɪm/ (practicing long /eɪ/ sound).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a writer says 'preserving old traditions is <i>essential</i> for our culture,' are they just stating a fact or sharing an opinion?" (Expected answer: Sharing an opinion.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we look for the writer's point of view, are we looking for general information or what the author *believes* or *argues*?" (Expected answer: What the author believes or argues.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive an authentic non-fiction text (e.g., an excerpt from an online article or an informational brochure) titled "Panama's Priceless Legacy: The Importance of Preservation Efforts." The text focuses on the <i>restoration</i> of Casco Viejo, the historical significance of the Panama Canal, and the cultural richness of indigenous <i>villages</i>, using words like <i>heritage, history, sites, tourists, culture, restoration, villages, landmark, legacy, identity, efforts, preserve, attract, live, offer, opened, became, transformed, continue, restore, play, relevant, historical, traditional, global, critical, cultural, significant, completed, interesting, restored, colonial, pre-hispanic</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will comprehend the main ideas and specific details in articles about cultural heritage, identifying different perspectives presented.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the text individually for two minutes and identify the main topic. Teacher asks: "What is this article primarily about?" (Expected answer: Panama's cultural heritage and the importance of preserving it.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Questions:</b> Students read specific sentences from the text and determine if they are true or false. Examples:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Casco Viejo, which is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, attracts tourists from around the world." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The Panama Canal has attracted many tourists since it opened in 1914, transforming global trade." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match key vocabulary words from the text (e.g., <i>heritage, restoration, legacy</i>) with their definitions or synonyms provided in a separate box.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice identifying the writer's point of view and understanding the use of relative clauses, past simple vs. present perfect, and future simple in the context of heritage preservation texts. Target vocabulary: <i>heritage, history, sites, tourists, culture, restoration, villages, trade routes, weaving, landmark, legacy, identity, efforts, preserve, attract, live, offer, opened, became, transformed, continue, restore, play, relevant, historical, traditional, global, critical, cultural, significant, completed, interesting, restored, colonial, pre-hispanic</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Grammar and Vocabulary):</b> Students complete a cloze exercise derived from the article. They fill in blanks using words from a provided list (target vocabulary: <i>preserve, became, heritage, restoration, attract, significant, global</i>) and choose the correct verb tense (Past Simple or Present Perfect) or relative pronoun (which/where).
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 1: "The ongoing ____ of Casco Viejo has been a ____ effort." (Answers: <i>restoration, significant</i>)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 2: "The Panama Canal ____ in 1914, ____ a pivotal part of Panama's ____ identity." (Answers: <i>opened, became, global</i>)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 3: "The Ngäbe-Buglé region, ____ their traditional festivals are celebrated, offers unique cultural experiences." (Answer: <i>where</i>)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Identifying Point of View):</b> Students reread specific paragraphs from the article. In pairs, they identify sentences that express the writer's point of view about heritage preservation. They underline these sentences and discuss what opinion the writer is conveying. Teacher provides sentence starters like: "The writer argues that..." or "The author believes that..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Textual Evidence):</b> Students discuss a specific question related to the text's argument. For example: "The article states that 'We will continue to <i>preserve</i> our cultural and historical <i>heritage</i>.' What evidence or examples does the writer offer in the text to support this prediction about future efforts?" Students must find and quote specific phrases or sentences as evidence. Students practice discussing respectfully: "I learned so much about the Emberá community when I visited their village, and it made me realize the importance of their traditions in preserving our national identity."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a short, additional non-fiction text (or a different section of the main article) about a specific Panamanian heritage site or tradition (e.g., Panama Viejo, Guna Yala's <i>weaving</i> traditions, the history of <i>trade routes</i>). The task is to read their assigned text carefully, identify the writer's main point of view regarding the preservation or significance of that heritage, and gather at least two pieces of textual evidence to support their finding.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Each group will prepare a brief (2-3 minute) oral summary and explanation of their text's writer's point of view to present to the class. They must use the target vocabulary and grammar structures where appropriate. Students should be ready to answer pragmatic questions like: "Why is <i>preserving</i> historic <i>landmarks</i> like Casco Viejo or the Panama Viejo ruins <i>important</i>?" and express sociolinguistic interest like: "Hearing about traditional festivals from the Ngäbe-Buglé region made me want to participate and understand their cultural roots."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, offering assistance with vocabulary and grammar, ensuring students are focusing on reading for viewpoint and supporting it with evidence. They encourage students to articulate their understanding using complex sentences, including relative clauses and different verb tenses.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and presentations, focusing on students' ability to identify the writer's point of view and support it with textual evidence.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students individually write a short paragraph (50-70 words) summarizing the main point of view from their group's text and providing one piece of textual evidence. They should try to incorporate at least two of the new vocabulary words and one relative clause or a sentence using Past Simple vs. Present Perfect. For example: "The author argues that preserving Casco Viejo is essential for maintaining Panama's cultural <i>legacy</i> and historical <i>identity</i>. They state, 'Casco Viejo, which is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, continues to <i>attract tourists</i> and has <i>transformed</i> the city's image.'"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to clearly identify the writer's point of view.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Provision of relevant textual evidence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (<i>heritage, legacy, identity, preserve, attract, restore</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of relative clauses (<i>which, where</i>) and verb tenses (Past Simple vs. Present Perfect).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the written summary.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new strategy I learned today to understand what a writer thinks is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I am more confident in identifying the main idea or the writer's argument when reading about cultural <i>heritage</i> because..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompts:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's lesson helped you most in recognizing a writer's point of view?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can identifying the writer's viewpoint help you better understand texts about important historical sites or traditions?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The focus on authentic non-fiction texts and specific strategies for identifying point of view seemed to engage students. The use of varied group texts allowed for diverse perspectives. The pronunciation practice of /eɪ/ in words like "trade" and "great," and reduced vowels in "important" and "heritage," was integrated naturally. Some students still struggled with distinguishing between a factual statement and an author's opinion, requiring further modeling and practice. For next lesson (Speaking & Interaction), I will plan activities that require students to orally express their own viewpoints on heritage preservation, building on today's reading comprehension skills and using the identified vocabulary and grammar structures. We will use phrases like "I believe that..." or "In my opinion..." to encourage expressive language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will discuss different viewpoints on heritage preservation, building on their ability to identify and analyze viewpoints from texts today. They will use the vocabulary and grammar to express their own opinions and engage in respectful dialogue about Panama's cultural <i>legacy</i>.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">2 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>